::: {.prose}
Virality is defined as the concept of being quickly and widely spread or popularized, especially by means of social media [@merriam_webster_viral]. With TikTok entering the scene around 2018 as a short-form video platform and gaining major widespread popularity, the music industry experienced a massive shift. Tiny snippets of songs with catchy lyrics or fun beats, can be shared in a single video and reach millions of fans. This in turn spikes listening and streaming of the song. It has truly changed music marketing. 


For some background, the Billboard Hot 100 is a music popularity ranking that originated in its current form in 1958. Published weekly, it is a list of the most popular singles, originally based on radio airplay and the sales of the song, but now incorporates streaming data as well. If you hear that a song is "Number 1," that means that it has reached the peak ranking of #1 on the Billboard Hot 100. This article examines the Billboard Hot 100 data from January 2016 to December 2025, for a total of 522 weeks, 5869 songs, and ____ artists, scraped using billboard.py. Below, we can see a visualization of the artists that had the most songs chart during this time period. 
:::

::: {.chart-wrap}
![](../../charts/num_songs_per_artist.svg)
[Your caption text here]{.caption}
:::

::: {.prose}
If a song remains popular, it can chart for many weeks, moving up and down the rankings. It can even drop off the chart and reappear if there is another spike in popularity. Below we have a vizualization of the longest charting songs over the past decade. These songs are examples of truly enduring hits that might not be the highest ranking song for all of its weeks on the chart, but they have consistent enough streams and sales in order to keep them in the top 100. 
:::

::: {.chart-wrap}
![](../../charts/num_weeks_per_song.svg)
[Diagram of number of weeks on the Billboard Hot 100 chart for the 20 most charting songs. The number of weeks represents the total number of weeks on the chart from 2016-2025, not necessarity consecutive weeks.]{.caption}
:::

::: {.prose}
In order to capture the true lifecycle of a song's experience on the Billboard Charts, however, we need to dive into the rankings it achieves. The following chart depicts every single song that charted between 2016 and 2025, distributed vertically by their peak ranking and horizontally by the number of weeks they charted. This number of weeks value is based only on the charts gathered between January 2016 and December 2025, and does not include the number of weeks a song may have been on the chart either before or after those dates. This plot shows that a majority of songs chart for fewer weeks, with the songs from the previous bar graph as outliers along the top right edge of the grid. The number of weeks a song charts for ranges from 1 to 112, but fifty percent of songs chart for only 3 weeks or less. There is a clear trend in this scatter plot that shows as songs are on the chart for more weeks, their peak rank generally increases. In fact, this relationship has a positive correlation of about 0.6. The peak rank of a song is also positively correlated with it's debut rank: the higher a song debut's on the Hot 100, the higher it's peak ranking. Click on any point in the graph below to view its ranking over time. 
:::

